{"id":"h209","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"claim","question":"Is the following claim true? Kurt Gödel published his incompleteness theorems in 1931.","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"author-written for this project; verified by the author","claims":[{"text":"Gödel's paper on formally undecidable propositions appeared in Monatshefte für Mathematik und Physik in 1931.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"The 1931 paper contains both the first and second incompleteness theorems.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"The standard publication date of the incompleteness theorems cited in textbooks and encyclopedias is 1931.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"The journal paper itself is dated 1931.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"Gödel first presented the results in September 1930 at a conference in Königsberg.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"One could argue the results were announced in 1930, making the 1931 date questionable.","confidence":0.15,"lean":-1,"theta":0.2356,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The question concerns publication, not announcement, so the 1930 talk does not undermine the claim.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"The 1931 journal publication settles the question of when the theorems were published.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradic